WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.129
A Torrance property owner proposes housing above shops on a wide commercial corridor.

2
00:00:05.129 --> 00:00:15.205
The use is allowed, but the project struggles with separate use rules, setbacks, parking ratios, driveway standards, and a review process designed for a different era.

3
00:00:15.205 --> 00:00:17.980
The question is not whether to abandon zoning.

4
00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:21.299
It is whether another tool fits the public goal better.

5
00:00:21.299 --> 00:00:25.533
This episode is preparation for civic planning work, not legal advice.

6
00:00:25.533 --> 00:00:28.860
California land use and housing laws change frequently.

7
00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:42.047
Any real regulation, permit, fee, incentive, or agreement should be evaluated under current statutes, constitutional limits, state guidance, adopted plans, local procedures, and advice from qualified staff and counsel.

8
00:00:42.047 --> 00:00:50.274
Conventional zoning usually organizes land by use and applies dimensional standards such as height, setbacks, lot coverage, and parking.

9
00:00:50.274 --> 00:00:53.661
It can provide predictability and separate harmful uses.

10
00:00:53.661 --> 00:01:00.557
It can also produce rigid categories, uniform districts, car-oriented form, and repeated discretionary exceptions.

11
00:01:00.557 --> 00:01:05.457
Moving beyond conventional zoning means building a larger implementation toolbox.

12
00:01:05.457 --> 00:01:07.876
The correct tool depends on the problem.

13
00:01:07.876 --> 00:01:10.720
If the concern is building form, regulate form.

14
00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:15.075
If it is noise, emissions, traffic, or stormwater, consider performance.

15
00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:20.096
If it is uncertain site conditions, a calibrated review process may be appropriate.

16
00:01:20.096 --> 00:01:26.326
If the goal is a coordinated district, a specific plan may integrate regulations and public investment.

17
00:01:26.326 --> 00:01:33.646
Form-based approaches emphasize the physical relationship among buildings, frontages, streets, blocks, and public spaces.

18
00:01:33.646 --> 00:01:40.058
Standards may address building placement, frontage type, height, entrances, transparency, and transitions.

19
00:01:40.058 --> 00:01:47.498
Uses still matter, especially where health and safety are involved, but compatible activities may share a broader category.

20
00:01:47.498 --> 00:01:51.309
Form standards can make the intended place easier to visualize.

21
00:01:51.309 --> 00:01:54.938
They can support walkable streets and predictable envelopes.

22
00:01:54.938 --> 00:01:59.596
They can also become overly prescriptive, expensive, or stylistically narrow.

23
00:01:59.596 --> 00:02:05.101
A code should regulate the public outcomes that matter, not freeze one architectural taste.

24
00:02:05.101 --> 00:02:08.065
Performance zoning focuses on measurable effects.

25
00:02:08.065 --> 00:02:17.743
Instead of banning every potentially problematic use, standards can address noise, glare, vibration, emissions, traffic, water quality, or operating conditions.

26
00:02:17.743 --> 00:02:20.284
This can allow flexibility and innovation.

27
00:02:20.284 --> 00:02:25.365
Performance systems need reliable measures, thresholds, monitoring, and enforcement.

28
00:02:25.365 --> 00:02:28.995
A standard that the city cannot test is not truly objective.

29
00:02:28.995 --> 00:02:32.503
Some effects vary over time or combine with other sources.

30
00:02:32.503 --> 00:02:37.705
Performance rules work best when the regulated impact can be defined and administered.

31
00:02:37.705 --> 00:02:41.762
Overlay zones add rules or incentives to an existing base district.

32
00:02:41.762 --> 00:02:47.332
A transit overlay might permit additional height or reduced parking near qualifying service.

33
00:02:47.332 --> 00:02:50.178
A hazard overlay might add safety requirements.

34
00:02:50.178 --> 00:02:53.690
A historic overlay might establish conservation standards.

35
00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:58.170
Overlays can target a special condition without rewriting the entire code.

36
00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:01.561
Too many overlays can create a difficult stack of rules.

37
00:03:01.561 --> 00:03:06.162
Every overlay should state which standard controls when provisions conflict.

38
00:03:06.162 --> 00:03:12.217
Incentive zoning offers additional development opportunity in exchange for a defined public benefit.

39
00:03:12.217 --> 00:03:17.484
Examples may involve affordable housing, public space, preservation, or infrastructure.

40
00:03:17.484 --> 00:03:24.810
California density bonus law creates state requirements that must be distinguished from a purely local incentive program.

41
00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.474
An incentive must be economically plausible.

42
00:03:27.474 --> 00:03:31.289
If the bonus has little value, it will not produce the benefit.

43
00:03:31.289 --> 00:03:38.312
If the requested benefit consumes more value than the incentive creates, the program may fail or raise legal issues.

44
00:03:38.312 --> 00:03:40.855
Periodic feasibility testing is essential.

45
00:03:40.855 --> 00:03:45.578
Planned development procedures can allow coordinated design for complex sites.

46
00:03:45.578 --> 00:03:50.664
They are useful where parcels, uses, infrastructure, or phasing require flexibility.

47
00:03:50.664 --> 00:03:52.480
Their weakness is uncertainty.

48
00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:58.111
If every standard becomes negotiable, outcomes depend on bargaining power and staff capacity.

49
00:03:58.111 --> 00:04:04.166
Establish baseline rules, approval criteria, public benefits, findings, and limits on modifications.

50
00:04:04.166 --> 00:04:07.980
Specific plans are powerful California tools for defined areas.

51
00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:14.580
They can combine land use, circulation, infrastructure, development standards, implementation, and financing.

52
00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:19.847
A specific plan can translate general plan policy into a coordinated district strategy.

53
00:04:19.847 --> 00:04:25.841
It requires significant preparation and should not become a way to postpone urgent citywide reform.

54
00:04:25.841 --> 00:04:32.017
Transfer of development rights separates development potential from the land that originally holds it.

55
00:04:32.017 --> 00:04:37.527
A sending area may preserve habitat, agriculture, historic resources, or hazard-prone land.

56
00:04:37.527 --> 00:04:40.433
A receiving area accepts additional development.

57
00:04:40.433 --> 00:04:48.062
A workable program needs legal clarity, demand, usable receiving capacity, transparent valuation, and administrative tracking.

58
00:04:48.062 --> 00:04:53.208
Cluster development groups buildings on part of a site while preserving another part.

59
00:04:53.208 --> 00:04:58.960
It can protect habitat, open space, or sensitive land without necessarily reducing total units.

60
00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:03.804
The preserved land needs durable ownership, management, and access arrangements.

61
00:05:03.804 --> 00:05:14.830
Inclusionary housing programs, affordable housing overlays, community benefit agreements, development agreements, impact fees, and public land strategies can also influence outcomes.

62
00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:17.010
These tools are not interchangeable.

63
00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:22.281
An impact fee funds facilities related to development under applicable legal standards.

64
00:05:22.281 --> 00:05:25.673
It should not be used as an informal price for approval.

65
00:05:25.673 --> 00:05:35.003
A development agreement can provide negotiated, long-term certainty and obligations, but it requires public findings and should not bypass broader policy.

66
00:05:35.003 --> 00:05:45.483
A community benefit agreement is generally a negotiated arrangement involving community groups and a developer; its parties and enforcement differ from municipal regulation.

67
00:05:45.483 --> 00:05:49.360
Design review is another tool, but it must have a clear purpose.

68
00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:55.600
Subjective review can improve a difficult proposal, yet it can also add cost, delay, and inconsistency.

69
00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:59.659
Objective design standards can address recurring issues in advance.

70
00:05:59.659 --> 00:06:06.868
Reserve discretionary design review for situations where contextual judgment is genuinely needed and legally available.

71
00:06:06.868 --> 00:06:09.533
Temporary and tactical tools can test ideas.

72
00:06:09.533 --> 00:06:16.257
A city might pilot curb management, a plaza, outdoor commerce, or a street redesign using reversible materials.

73
00:06:16.257 --> 00:06:22.012
A pilot should have a purpose, duration, safety plan, evaluation measures, and a decision rule.

74
00:06:22.012 --> 00:06:27.889
Temporary status should not become an excuse for avoiding accessibility or meaningful engagement.

75
00:06:27.889 --> 00:06:31.039
Tool selection should follow a disciplined sequence.

76
00:06:31.039 --> 00:06:33.038
First, define the public problem.

77
00:06:33.038 --> 00:06:36.249
Second, identify the authority and legal constraints.

78
00:06:36.249 --> 00:06:38.854
Third, choose the narrowest effective tool.

79
00:06:38.854 --> 00:06:42.064
Fourth, test feasibility and administrative capacity.

80
00:06:42.064 --> 00:06:44.427
Fifth, evaluate distributional effects.

81
00:06:44.427 --> 00:06:47.032
Sixth, establish monitoring and correction.

82
00:06:47.032 --> 00:06:49.697
Administrative capacity is often overlooked.

83
00:06:49.697 --> 00:06:54.786
A sophisticated performance code may fail if the city lacks equipment or inspectors.

84
00:06:54.786 --> 00:06:59.754
A transfer program may generate no transactions if receiving areas are infeasible.

85
00:06:59.754 --> 00:07:03.752
A highly discretionary process may overwhelm staff and applicants.

86
00:07:03.752 --> 00:07:07.871
Simplicity is a public value when it improves compliance and access.

87
00:07:07.871 --> 00:07:16.534
For the Torrance mixed-use corridor, suppose the goal is housing production, active frontages, safer walking, and continued commercial service.

88
00:07:16.534 --> 00:07:33.194
A combined approach might use a form-based frontage standard, broader compatible-use categories, objective transitions near homes, reduced parking requirements tied to context, performance limits for noise and loading, and a specific capital plan for sidewalks and crossings.

89
00:07:33.194 --> 00:07:36.889
The city should compare this package with simpler amendments.

90
00:07:36.889 --> 00:07:43.129
Perhaps existing zoning can work after removing one parking minimum and clarifying mixed-use standards.

91
00:07:43.129 --> 00:07:47.369
Innovation should solve a demonstrated problem, not advertise novelty.

92
00:07:47.369 --> 00:07:50.095
Monitor both intended and unintended effects.

93
00:07:50.095 --> 00:07:51.247
Are homes approved?

94
00:07:51.247 --> 00:07:53.851
Are storefronts usable by small businesses?

95
00:07:53.851 --> 00:07:55.669
Do loading conflicts increase?

96
00:07:55.669 --> 00:07:58.577
Are projects concentrated only on large parcels?

97
00:07:58.577 --> 00:08:01.787
Do standards add cost without visible public benefit?

98
00:08:01.787 --> 00:08:03.726
Is displacement pressure rising?

99
00:08:03.726 --> 00:08:06.876
Regulation should be adjusted when evidence changes.

100
00:08:06.876 --> 00:08:09.666
The learner artifact is a tool-selection memo.

101
00:08:09.666 --> 00:08:12.092
Choose one planning problem in Torrance.

102
00:08:12.092 --> 00:08:15.609
Describe the desired public outcome without naming a tool.

103
00:08:15.609 --> 00:08:22.887
Compare conventional use zoning, a form standard, a performance standard, an overlay, an incentive, and a specific plan.

104
00:08:22.887 --> 00:08:27.011
For each, write one strength, one risk, and one administrative need.

105
00:08:27.011 --> 00:08:29.922
Recommend a package of no more than three tools.

106
00:08:29.922 --> 00:08:38.594
Explain why each is necessary, how conflicts will be resolved, what equity effect requires attention, and what evidence would trigger revision.

107
00:08:38.594 --> 00:08:39.989
Three recall questions.

108
00:08:39.989 --> 00:08:44.477
First, when is a form-based standard more direct than conventional zoning?

109
00:08:44.477 --> 00:08:47.570
Second, what makes a performance standard workable?

110
00:08:47.570 --> 00:08:51.148
Third, why must incentive zoning be tested for feasibility?

111
00:08:51.148 --> 00:09:01.963
First answer: it is more direct when the main public concern is building placement, frontage, scale, transitions, and the relationship between private development and public space.

112
00:09:01.963 --> 00:09:10.915
Second answer: the effect must be measurable, the threshold clear, monitoring practical, cumulative conditions considered, and enforcement available.

113
00:09:10.915 --> 00:09:19.387
Third answer: the additional development value must be sufficient and usable, or the incentive will not produce the requested public benefit.

114
00:09:19.387 --> 00:09:36.232
Source note: This original narration draws on the California Governor’s Office of Land Use and Climate Innovation 2017 General Plan Guidelines and current technical advisories concerning implementation, specific plans, housing, complete streets, safety, and environmental justice.

115
00:09:36.232 --> 00:09:42.068
The 2017 guidelines remain the current comprehensive edition while LCI develops updated guidance.

116
00:09:42.068 --> 00:09:50.189
Current California statutes control, particularly where housing approvals, density bonuses, fees, and objective standards are involved.

117
